WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.640
Show your product, pitch to shops, and plan 90 days.

2
00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:08.100
This is the video for Module 4 of Product Packaging and Branding.

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:23.200
Over this course you have made many pieces: a brand kit, label versions, a costing sheet, a test log and a compliance audit. Now pull them into one place. Create one folder in Google Drive for the project.

4
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:31.150
Inside, make subfolders, such as Brand, Labels, Photos, Costing, Tests, Compliance and Report.

5
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:40.800
Name each file with brand, item and version, like Ruby Zobo label version 3. Avoid names like final final 2.

6
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:47.620
Keep only the latest approved label in the Labels folder, so a printer never gets the wrong file.

7
00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.220
And share it as view only when you send it outside your business.

8
00:00:53.470 --> 00:01:03.620
Zainab had notes and photos scattered across her phone and WhatsApp chats. In one afternoon, she created her folder and saved her brand kit and print-ready label.

9
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:15.200
She typed her paper costing sheet into Google Sheets. Packaging is about 145 naira, and her full cost is about 610 naira per unit.

10
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:23.280
She sells wholesale at about 870 naira, with a retail price of about 1,100.

11
00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:29.730
Later, when a supermarket buyer asked for her product details, she sent one folder link in a minute.

12
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:40.850
Many buyers first meet your product as a photo on WhatsApp. Set a table beside a window with indirect daylight. Avoid harsh midday sun and yellow bulbs.

13
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.390
Use a plain background, like white cardboard curved up behind the product.

14
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.740
Wipe off fingerprints and dust, straighten the label, and clean the lens.

15
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:57.850
Do not use digital zoom. Move the phone closer and tap the label to focus.

16
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:07.610
Take three shots: the front at eye level, a three-quarter angle, and the back label. Do not change the colours, so the real product matches.

17
00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:19.510
For example, Bisi takes two jars of groundnut butter and a one-page sell sheet to a supermarket in Ibadan. She greets the buyer and introduces Golden Spread.

18
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.630
She says what it is: groundnut butter in 250 g jars, with no added sugar.

19
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:37.110
She gives his numbers. His price is 1,900 naira, it sells at 2,500, so he keeps 600 naira per jar.

20
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.630
She makes a small offer: 12 jars this week, on sale or return for the first month.

21
00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:56.400
Then she asks what he would need before stocking it. He says customers ask for NAFDAC numbers, and the till needs a barcode. She writes it down, and follows up on WhatsApp that evening.

22
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:07.960
Before you leave goods on sale or return, agree in writing how many units, the price, when you will check stock, and when unsold or expired goods come back.

23
00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.300
Keep copies of every delivery note.

24
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:24.770
And use only official NAFDAC, CAC and GS1 Nigeria channels. A number someone arranges quickly outside the official process can be invalid and put your business at risk.

25
00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:37.480
A good plan has a few actions, each with a date, a cost, and a way to know it is done. For example, Funke has a tested label for Ruby Zobo, but no registration yet.

26
00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:45.150
In days 1 to 30, she registers her business name with CAC and prepares her production space.

27
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:53.270
In days 31 to 60, she submits her NAFDAC application and applies for GS1 barcodes.

28
00:03:54.520 --> 00:04:06.960
In days 61 to 90, she pitches to three cafes and one supermarket, aiming for 2 new outlets and 70 bottles a week. She sells 50 a week now, so it is a stretch, but possible.

29
00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:13.180
On day 90, she compares her scorecard with her baseline and updates her report.

30
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:21.270
Each step depends on the one before. She does not print a large label run until her NAFDAC number is issued.

31
00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:38.820
To remember. Keep your project in one clear folder. Photograph in daylight. Pitch with the shop's numbers, and follow up within a week. Agree terms in writing. And set targets you can count, like 70 bottles a week by day 90, not just sell more.

32
00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:52.890
Now assemble your project folder, photograph your package and pitch to one real shop, then write your reflection and 90-day plan. You need at least 70% in the final quiz for your certificate.

33
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.190
Continue with the lessons below.
